## Appendix A Further implementation details

In this section, we describe additional implementation details to enable Algorithm 1 to run in time which scales linearly in the number of events that occur in the full population. First, we must implement GETNEXTEVENT in constant time in the population size, which is described in Appendix A.1. We must also store, at any given time, the active population in a data structure that supports insertion, deletion, and selection of an element uniformly at random, in constant time in the population size, which is described in Appendix A.2. Finally, our ability to implement GETNEXTEVENT requires we perform precomputations before simulation time. These are described in Appendix A.3.

### A.1 Implementing GETNEXTEVENT in constant time in the population size

As described in Section 3.1, in order for Algorithm 1 to have time complexity linear in the number of events in the full phylogeny, we must implement the function GETNEXTEVENT in constant time in the population size. Our implementation relies on the following fact. Consider a Poisson point process with time-varying rate given by $\theta(\tau)\geq 0$ . Let

$$ \Theta(\tau,\tau^{\prime}):=\int_{\tau}^{\tau^{\prime}}\theta(\tau)\mathrm{d}\tau,\qquad\Theta^{-1}(\tau,z):=\sup\{\tau^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}\mid\Theta(\tau,\tau^{\prime})\leq z\}. $$

Consider $n$ iid copies of this Poisson point process. For a fixed time $\tau$ , consider the next time after $\tau$ that an event occurs in any one of these $n$ processes. It is routine to show that this arrival time has distribution given by $\Theta^{-1}(\tau,Z/n)$ where $Z\sim\mathrm{Exp}(1)$ has exponential distribution with rate parameter $1$ (that is, has density given by $e^{-z}$ for $z\geq 0$ ). Note that $\Theta(\tau,Z/n)$ can be infinite if $\lim_{\tau^{\prime}\rightarrow\infty}\Theta(\tau,\tau^{\prime})<\infty$ and $Z/n\geq\lim_{\tau^{\prime}\rightarrow\infty}\Theta(\tau,\tau^{\prime})$ . This corresponds to the event that none of the $n$ Poisson processes have any events occur after time $\tau$ , which can occur when the Poisson rate function is integrable.

The GETNEXTEVENT function takes in the current time $\tau$ and the number of extant lineages of each type $|S_{a}|$ . Given the discussion in the previous paragraph, this provides a way of determining when the next event in the population occurs, and what type it is, where the type specifies whether it is a birth, death, mutation, or sampling event, and the types of the parent and child nodes in the phylogeny. For each event type, we generate the time of the next event of that type. The next event type is chosen to be the one that happens the soonest among these generated times. Formally, for $a,b=1,\dots,d$ , define

$$ \begin{gathered}\Lambda_{a,b}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})=\int_{\tau_{1}}^{\tau_{2}}\lambda_{a,b}(\tau)\mathrm{d}\tau,\qquad M_{a}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})=\int_{\tau_{1}}^{\tau_{2}}\mu_{a}(\tau)\mathrm{d}\tau,\\
\Gamma_{a,b}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})=\int_{\tau_{1}}^{\tau_{2}}\gamma_{a,b}(\tau)\mathrm{d}\tau,\qquad\Psi_{a}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})=\int_{\tau_{1}}^{\tau_{2}}\psi_{a}(\tau)\mathrm{d}\tau.\end{gathered} $$

Then define

$$ \begin{gathered}\Lambda_{a,b}^{-1}(\tau,z)=\sup\{\tau^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}\mid\Lambda_{a,b}(\tau,\tau^{\prime})\leq z\},\qquad M_{a}^{-1}(\tau_{1},z)=\sup\{\tau^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}\mid M_{a}(\tau,\tau^{\prime})\leq z\}\\
\Gamma_{a,b}^{-1}(\tau_{1},z)=\sup\{\tau^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}\mid\Gamma_{a,b}(\tau,\tau^{\prime})\leq z\}\qquad\Psi_{a}^{-1}(\tau_{1},z)=\sup\{\tau^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}\mid\Psi_{a}(\tau,\tau^{\prime})\leq z\},\end{gathered} $$

where these can be possibly infinite (for example, if one of the rate parameters is 0). We draw exponential random variables $\{Z_{a,b}^{\gamma}\}_{a,b=1}^{d}$ , $\{Z_{a}^{\mu}\}_{a=1}^{d}$ , $\{Z_{a,b}^{\gamma}\}_{a,b=1}^{d}$ , $\{Z_{a}^{\psi}\}_{a=1}^{d}$ iid from $\mathrm{Exp}(1)$ so that we have one exponential random variable for each event type. The occurrence of the next birth event $a\rightarrow a,b$ occurs at time $\Lambda_{a,b}^{-1}(\tau,Z_{a,b}^{\lambda}/|S_{a}|)$ ; the next death of a lineage with type $a$ occurs at time $M_{a}^{-1}(\tau,Z_{a}^{\mu}/|S_{a}|)$ ; the next anagenetic mutation $a\rightarrow b$ occurs at time $\Gamma_{a,b}^{-1}(\tau,Z_{a,b}^{\gamma}/|S_{a}|)$ ; and the next sampling event occurs at $\Psi_{a}^{-1}(\tau,Z_{a}^{\psi}/|S_{a}|)$ . The next event of any type occurs at the minimum of these, and the type of the next event is determined by which process achieves the minimum. Thus, GETNEXTEVENT can be implemented by the pseudo-code in Algorithm 3.

